\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Finite-rate frequency deficit $\Delta f_{\rm  peak}=(1-X_q)\,\Delta f_{\rm  PT}$ from Eqs.~\eqref  {eq:lobe_fraction} and \eqref  {eq:rate_shift}, using the pre-existing-core geometry with $R_0=1\,\text  {km}$, $R_{\rm  lobe}=3\,\text  {km}$, and $\Delta t_{\rm  pm}=10\,\text  {ms}$. Each curve uses a $\Delta f_{\rm  PT}$ calibrated as the endpoint difference between hybrid-EOS and purely hadronic instantaneous-conversion simulations, labeled by EOS pair and binary masses; five cases (all BLh/BLQ pairs and the DD2F/DD2F-SF1 $1.289{+}1.289$ pair) are from Table IV of \cite  {Prakash:2023afe}, and the DD2F/DD2F-SF1 $1.35{+}1.35$ pair is from \cite  {Bauswein:2018bma}. The two BLh/BLQ binaries with $\Delta f_{\rm  PT}=0.123\,\text  {kHz}$ overlap. Neither reference reports the size of the quark-matter region, so we apply the same fiducial geometry to all six cases. The converted fraction $X_q(u(0^-))$ is then common to every curve and $\Delta f_{\rm  PT}$ only rescales it, which is why the curves fall off together: the location of the fall-off is set by the geometry alone, not by the equation of state. The grey band on the left marks the maximum boundary speeds calculated in Sec.~\ref {sec:results} at merger temperatures. The dashed line is the saturation speed $u_{\rm  sat}=(R_{\rm  lobe}-R_0)/\Delta t_{\rm  pm}$, above which the front would fill the lobes within the emission window, and the light band around it shows how far $u_{\rm  sat}$ moves for $R_{\rm  lobe}=2$--$4\,\text  {km}$ and $\Delta t_{\rm  pm}=5$--$20\,\text  {ms}$. Throughout the shaded band the deficit is $(1-X_q)\,\Delta f_{\rm  PT}\approx 0.96\,\Delta f_{\rm  PT}=0.12$--$0.45\,\text  {kHz}$, above the $\delta f_{\rm  min}\approx 0.1\,\text  {kHz}$ resolution of a loud (postmerger signal-to-noise ratio ${\gtrsim }10$) next-generation detection \cite  {Breschi:2022ens,Clark:2015zxa}.}}{3}{}}
\newlabel{fig:rate_deficit}{{2}{3}{}{}{}}
